    "details": {
      "alignment": "neutral",
      "type": {
        "value": "humanoid",
        "subtype": "koopa"
      },
      "cr": 3,
      "biography": {
        "value": "<h1>Hammer Bro — The Elite Who Earned It</h1><p>Hammer Bros are the Koopa Troop's elite infantry—koopas who distinguished themselves through combat performance and were promoted to a specialized role that combines armored durability with ranged firepower through the ancient koopa art of throwing hammers at things until those things stop being problems. They wear helmets that standard Troopas don't receive, carry an apparently infinite supply of hammers that they produce from somewhere nobody has ever adequately explained, and operate in paired teams that coordinate their throws to create overlapping fields of hammers that make approaching them an exercise in pain avoidance.</p><p>The hammers are the mystery. Hammer Bros throw hammers continuously during combat—not one or two, but DOZENS, an unending stream of spinning metal that arcs over intervening obstacles and descends on targets with trajectories that defy simple ballistics. Where the hammers come from is the subject of intense speculation among Mushroom Kingdom intelligence analysts. Some theories suggest hammerspace—a pocket dimension from which the hammers are summoned. Others suggest rapid materialization through innate magic that Hammer Bros themselves don't understand. The most prosaic theory involves hidden compartments in the shell, which would explain the hammers but not the apparent unlimited supply.</p><p>Hammer Bros are proud. They earned their helmets through performance, and they maintain their status through continued excellence. A Hammer Bro who misses consistently is demoted to standard Troopa duty, which is considered humiliating enough to motivate accuracy that borders on supernatural. They work in pairs because paired Hammer Bros are exponentially more dangerous than individual ones—while one throws, the other repositions, creating a rotating barrage that never stops and never comes from the same angle twice.</p>",
        "public": "A helmeted koopa with an expression of professional determination and a hammer in each hand, with more hammers visible somewhere behind him in quantities that shouldn't be possible."
      },
